WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.234 --> 00:00:05.766
What happens when a country treats public health as a residual cost instead of an economic engine?

2
00:00:06.314 --> 00:00:10.278
Traditionally, governments fund health care only after economic growth is secured.

3
00:00:10.762 --> 00:00:15.334
But in Bangladesh, public health allocation sits at less than 1% of GDP.

4
00:00:15.722 --> 00:00:18.278
This creates a brutal mathematical consequence.

5
00:00:18.602 --> 00:00:22.758
A 68% out-of-pocket burden shifted directly onto citizens.

6
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:24.102
The human fallout?

7
00:00:24.554 --> 00:00:28.454
Nearly five million people are pushed into poverty every year paying for basic care.

8
00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:32.774
This is a systemic failure, not just a humanitarian crisis.

9
00:00:33.194 --> 00:00:35.558
When citizens are bankrupted by medical bills,

10
00:00:35.786 --> 00:00:38.950
untreated illnesses rise and the healthy workforce fractures.

11
00:00:39.402 --> 00:00:42.758
That's why health system resilience is actually economic protection.

12
00:00:43.274 --> 00:00:46.726
A healthy workforce is the foundational infrastructure of productivity.

13
00:00:47.178 --> 00:00:51.238
Which means public health spending generates economic returns rather than consuming them.

14
00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:54.406
A resilient health system doesn't just save lives.

15
00:00:54.698 --> 00:00:56.422
It protects the entire economy.

16
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.494
Human in purpose. Systemic in method.

17
00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:01.216
Protective in outcome.
